    "id": "hf-6035",
    "crop": "Tomato",
    "instruction": "How can I prevent blossom end rot in tomato plants?",
    "advisory": "Blossom end rot is caused by calcium deficiency or uneven soil moisture, resulting in dark, sunken lesions at the bottom of tomato fruits. Prevent blossom end rot by maintaining consistent soil moisture through regular watering and mulching. Ensure adequate calcium availability by applying gypsum or calcium-rich fertilizers to the soil before planting. Avoid over-fertilizing with nitrogen, as it can interfere with calcium uptake.",
