    "id": "hf-7560",
    "crop": "Tomato",
    "instruction": "The leaves of tomato crop can dry up due to various reasons. Here are some possible causes:\n\n- Overwatering: Excessive irrigation can lead to wilting and drying of tomato leaves. It is important to avoid overwatering and maintain a proper watering schedule.\n\n- Leaf curl virus: Overwatering can increase the risk of leaf curl virus, which can cause the leaves to dry up. Proper irrigation practices can help prevent this virus.\n\n- Fusarium fungus: Fusarium fungus can turn the roots brown or black, leading to yellowing and drying of the plants, including the leaves. Proper crop rotation, seed treatment, and irrigation management can help control this fungus.\n\n- Powdery mildew: Powdery mildew can affect the leaves, causing them to shrink and dry. It spreads rapidly in dry weather and lower temperatures. Spraying with Myclobutanil or soluble Sulphur can help control this disease.\n\nPossible follow-up questions:\n1. How can I prevent leaf curl virus in tomato plants?\n2. What are the symptoms of Fusarium fungus in tomato crops?\n3. Are there any natural remedies for controlling powdery mildew in tomatoes?",
