    "crop": "Mustard",
    "instruction": "Thinning is an important practice in farming mustard. Here's how you can do it:\n- After 12-15 days of sowing, thin the dense seedlings.\n- Maintain a distance of 10-15 centimeters between the plants.\n- Include one round of weeding along with thinning to control diseases.\n- Apply 3.3 liters of Pendimethalin 30 EC per hectare, diluted in 800-1000 liters of water, immediately after sowing and before the onset of cold weather.\n\nIrrigation management is crucial for yellow mustard. Here are some tips:\n- Yellow mustard is sensitive to water deficiency before flowering.\n- Adequate irrigation should be ensured for good yields.\n- Proper water drainage should also be maintained.\n\nTo control aphids in mustard, you can take the following measures:\n- Aphids are small insects that feed on the leaves of mustard plants.\n- They make holes in the leaves from the edges or in the middle.\n- During severe infestations, the entire plant becomes devoid of leaves.\n- To control aphids, you can use appropriate insecticides recommended for mustard crops.\n\nHere are some follow-up questions you can ask:\n1. How can I prevent aphid infestations in my mustard crop?\n2. Are there any natural methods to control aphids in mustard?\n3. Can you recommend any specific insecticides for aphid control in mustard?",
