    "crop": "Mango",
    "instruction": "It is possible that mango trees do not flower even when the tree is big due to various factors such as nutrient deficiencies, environmental conditions, or improper care. Here are some possible reasons and solutions:\n\n1. Nutrient deficiencies: Lack of certain nutrients like zinc, boron, or manganese can affect flowering in mango trees. Spraying zinc sulphate (0.3%) during February, March, and May can correct zinc deficiency. Spraying Borax (0.5%) after fruit set twice at monthly intervals and 0.5% manganese sulphate after blooming can correct boron and manganese deficiencies respectively.\n\n2. Environmental conditions: Factors like high humidity, rain, or frost during flowering can hinder the flowering process. Mango trees thrive in areas with a temperature between 24 and 27°C during fruit development and maturity. Ensure that the climate conditions are suitable for mango cultivation.\n\n3. Improper care: Inadequate irrigation or improper pruning and training practices can also affect flowering. Young plants up to 2 years old should be watered regularly, and newly-planted grafts need about 30 liters of water every week. Irrigation during the preflowering phase and after fruit set at a 10-day interval can promote flowering and increase yield. Proper training and pruning techniques should be followed to facilitate intercultural operations.\n\nPossible follow-up questions:\n1. How can I determine if my mango tree has nutrient deficiencies?\n2. What are the ideal environmental conditions for mango flowering?\n3. Can you provide more information on proper pruning and training techniques for mango trees?",
